title: Proposition 4
---
order: 4
---
short_label: II.4
---
sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: If a straight line is cut at random, the square on the whole equals the squares on the segments plus twice the rectangle contained by the segments.
----
proof:

Let the straight line {AB} be cut at random at {C}.

I say that the square on {AB} equals the sum of the squares on {AC} and {CB} plus twice the rectangle {AC} by {CB}.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="230" height="220" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img src="propII4.gif" alt="II.4"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // Joyce's figure draws the two squares HF and CK but NOT the two equal
    // rectangles AG and GE that the proof spends its last third on, and not the
    // segments AC, CB, CG, GK, KB, HG it names throughout. Almost everything
    // added here is fully transparent (;0;0;0 lines, ;0;0;0;0 polygons) or an
    // angle marker (hidden in the static figure since 0.9.0), so the resting
    // figure is unchanged. The two exceptions are commented where they are
    // declared: the black AB, and the two initiallyHidden rectangles.
    //
    // Colour: the two squares on the segments take stable pastels, and the two
    // rectangles — equal to each other, the "twice the rectangle" of the
    // enunciation — SHARE one colour. The containing square ADEB is left
    // unfilled so its own four parts fill it.
    //
    // NOTE on BADE: it carries the DEFAULT faceColor (only four ;-fields), i.e.
    // lighten(bgcolor) — a near-white OPAQUE face, not transparency. That white
    // is visible in Joyce's resting figure (it is why the AG and GE corners read
    // white rather than the page's peach), so it stays. But a highlighted
    // polygon is promoted within its draw pass (#140), so highlighting BADE
    // would white out the whole decomposition on the closing slides — the
    // I.41/I.44/I.47 bug. BADE is therefore NEVER named in a slide set; the
    // fully transparent ADEB below is the highlight/hover target for the whole.
    var CUT   = ["A","B","C","AB","AC","CB"];
    var BUILT = CUT.concat(["BADE","D","E","F","G","H","K",
                            "AD","BE","DE","BD","CF","HK","ADEB"]);
    var PARL  = BUILT.concat(["angCGB","angADB"]);
    var ISOSC = PARL.concat(["angABD"]);
    var EQANG = ISOSC.concat(["CG"]);
    var EQUIL = EQANG.concat(["GK","KB","CBKG"]);
    var RTANG = EQUIL.concat(["angKBC","angGCB"]);
    var RTALL = RTANG.concat(["angCGK","angGKB"]);
    var AREAS = BUILT.concat(["CG","GK","KB","CBKG","DFGH","HG"]);
    var TWICE = AREAS.concat(["ACGH","GKEF"]);
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "II.4",
        elements: [
            "A0;point;free;-1000,40;0,0",
            "A3;point;free;1000,40;0,0",
            "A;point;lineSlider;A0,A3,40,0",
            "B;point;lineSlider;A0,A3,190,0",
            "C;point;lineSlider;A0,A3,130,0",
            "BADE;polygon;square;B,A",
            "E;point;vertex;BADE,4",
            "D;point;vertex;BADE,3",
            "BD;line;connect;B,D",
            "F;point;parallelogram;D,A,C",
            "CF;line;connect;C,F",
            "G;point;intersection;C,F,B,D",
            // The two squares on the segments. Joyce's params gave both a
            // `random` face — a fresh hue on every load, which in a deck whose
            // argument is "equal regions share a colour" can roll up the same
            // salmon as the two rectangles below and assert an equality that
            // does not hold. Fixed pastels instead; same family, so they still
            // read as the pair of squares on AC and CB.
            "DFGH;polygon;parallelogram;D,F,G;0;0;0;palegreen",
            "H;point;vertex;DFGH,4",
            "K;point;intersection;G,H,B,E",
            "HK;line;connect;H,K",
            "CBKG;polygon;quadrilateral;C,B,K,G;0;0;0;lightblue",
            // AB is drawn BLACK, not transparent: slide 1 is the cut line
            // alone, before the square exists, so there has to be something to
            // see. It coincides pixel-for-pixel with the square's own top edge,
            // so the resting figure is unchanged.
            "AB;line;connect;A,B",
            // Hover / highlight targets only — the square, CF and HK already
            // draw every one of these.
            "AC;line;connect;A,C;0;0;0",
            "CB;line;connect;C,B;0;0;0",
            "AD;line;connect;A,D;0;0;0",
            "BE;line;connect;B,E;0;0;0",
            "DE;line;connect;D,E;0;0;0",
            "CG;line;connect;C,G;0;0;0",
            "GK;line;connect;G,K;0;0;0",
            "KB;line;connect;K,B;0;0;0",
            "HG;line;connect;H,G;0;0;0",
            // The two rectangles the proof calls AG and GE. Equal to each
            // other, so one colour between them. initiallyHidden, so Joyce's
            // resting figure keeps its two white corners.
            "ACGH;polygon;quadrilateral;A,C,G,H;0;0;0;lightsalmon",
            "GKEF;polygon;quadrilateral;G,K,E,F;0;0;0;lightsalmon",
            // The whole square, which the proof calls ADEB. Unfilled: its four
            // parts carry the colour. This, not BADE, is what the slides light.
            "ADEB;polygon;quadrilateral;A,D,E,B;0;0;0;0",
            // Angles. angABD doubles as GBC (G lies on BD, C on BA — same
            // rays), and angGCB as BCG; #103 auto-nests the two same-vertex
            // pairs (at G, CGB inside CGK; at B, ABD inside KBC).
            "angCGB;sector;angleMarker;G,C,B",
            "angADB;sector;angleMarker;D,A,B",
            "angABD;sector;angleMarker;B,A,D",
            "angKBC;sector;angleMarker;B,K,C",
            "angGCB;sector;angleMarker;C,G,B",
            "angCGK;sector;angleMarker;G,C,K",
            "angGKB;sector;angleMarker;K,G,B"
        ],
        // No deferDraggables. The only free / slider points are the off-canvas
        // carriers A0 and A3 (drawn with no name and no vertex) and the three
        // sliders A, B and C — and slide 1 introduces all three, so the
        // auto-union is right here. The rest of the figure (D, E, F, G, H, K)
        // is derived and therefore NOT auto-unioned: each is listed explicitly
        // in every slide it belongs to, from BUILT on.
        initiallyHidden: ["ACGH","GKEF"],
        aliases: { "HF": "DFGH", "CK": "CBKG", "KC": "CBKG", "CGKB": "CBKG",
                   "AG": "ACGH", "GE": "GKEF",
                   "BA": "AB", "BC": "CB", "GC": "CG", "BK": "KB", "EB": "BE",
                   "CGB": "angCGB", "ADB": "angADB", "ABD": "angABD",
                   "GBC": "angABD", "KBC": "angKBC", "GCB": "angGCB",
                   "BCG": "angGCB", "CGK": "angCGK", "GKB": "angGKB" },
        slides: [
            { text: "Let the straight line {AB} be cut at random at {C}.",
              visible: CUT,
              highlighted: ["AB","C"] },
            { text: "I say that the square on {AB} equals the sum of the squares on {AC} and {CB} plus twice the rectangle {AC} by {CB}.",
              visible: CUT,
              highlighted: ["AC","CB"] },
            { text: "Describe the square {ADEB} on {AB}. Join {BD}. Draw {CF} through {C} parallel to either {AD} or {EB}, and draw {HK} through {G} parallel to either {AB} or {DE}.",
              visible: BUILT,
              // Deliberately NOT highlighted: these four are the animation's own
              // targets. SlateAnimator hides an animated target until its cascade
              // step starts (#83) but sets shouldHighlight at run start, and
              // drawFace draws a hidden element that is highlighted — so listing
              // them paints the whole construction from t=0. The animation is
              // the emphasis (euclid#171).
              highlighted: [],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "BADE", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outline },
                  { elem: "BD",   name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect },
                  { elem: "CF",   name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect },
                  { elem: "HK",   name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect } ] },
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "the square ADEB on AB",    ref: "I.46" },
                  { claim: "CF and HK drawn parallel", ref: "I.31" } ] },
            { text: "Then, since {CF} is parallel to {AD}, and {BD} falls on them, the exterior angle {CGB} equals the interior and opposite angle {ADB}.",
              visible: PARL,
              highlighted: ["CF","AD","BD"],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "angCGB", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep },
                  { elem: "angADB", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] },
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "exterior CGB = interior opposite ADB", ref: "I.29" } ] },
            { text: "But the angle {ADB} equals the angle {ABD}, since the side {BA} also equals {AD}.",
              visible: ISOSC,
              // angABD is this slide's animation target (euclid#171).
              highlighted: ["angADB","AB","AD"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "angABD", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] },
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "ADB = ABD, since BA = AD", ref: "I.5" } ] },
            { text: "Therefore the angle {CGB} also equals the angle {GBC}, so that the side {BC} also equals the side {CG}.",
              visible: EQANG,
              highlighted: ["angCGB","angABD","CB","CG"],
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "equal angles give BC = CG", ref: "I.6" } ] },
            { text: "But {CB} equals {GK}, and {CG} to {KB}. Therefore {GK} also equals {KB}. Therefore {CGKB} is equilateral.",
              visible: EQUIL,
              // CBKG is this slide's animation target (euclid#171).
              highlighted: ["CB","GK","CG","KB"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "CBKG", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outlineAndFill } ] },
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "opposite sides CB = GK, CG = KB", ref: "I.34" } ] },
            { text: "I say next that it is also right-angled.",
              visible: EQUIL,
              highlighted: ["CBKG"] },
            { text: "Since {CG} is parallel to {BK}, the sum of the angles {KBC} and {GCB} equals two right angles.",
              visible: RTANG,
              highlighted: ["CG","KB"],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "angKBC", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep },
                  { elem: "angGCB", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] },
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "KBC + GCB = two right angles", ref: "I.29" } ] },
            { text: "But the angle {KBC} is right. Therefore the angle {BCG} is also right, so that the opposite angles {CGK} and {GKB} are also right.",
              visible: RTALL,
              highlighted: ["angKBC","angGCB"],
              // parallel: CGK and GKB are the opposite pair, carried by one step.
              transition: { mode: "parallel", animations: [
                  { elem: "angCGK", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep },
                  { elem: "angGKB", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] },
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "opposite angles CGK and GKB are right", ref: "I.34" } ] },
            { text: "Therefore {CGKB} is right-angled, and it was also proved equilateral, therefore it is a square, and it is described on {CB}.",
              visible: RTALL,
              highlighted: ["CBKG","CB"],
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "equilateral and right-angled, so a square", ref: "I.Def.22" } ] },
            { text: "For the same reason {HF} is also a square, and it is described on {HG}, that is {AC}. Therefore the squares {HF} and {KC} are the squares on {AC} and {CB}.",
              visible: AREAS,
              // DFGH (HF) is this slide's animation target (euclid#171).
              highlighted: ["HG","AC","CB","CBKG"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "DFGH", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outlineAndFill } ] },
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "opposite sides HG = AC", ref: "I.34" } ] },
            { text: "Now, since {AG} equals {GE}, and {AG} is the rectangle {AC} by {CB}, for {GC} equals {CB}, "
                  + "therefore {GE} also equals the rectangle {AC} by {CB}.",
              visible: TWICE,
              // ACGH (AG) and GKEF (GE) are this slide's animation targets
              // (euclid#171); GC and CB, which the step turns on, are not.
              highlighted: ["CG","CB"],
              // parallel: the two are equal, and the point is that they arrive
              // together — one rectangle taken twice.
              transition: { mode: "parallel", animations: [
                  { elem: "ACGH", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outlineAndFill },
                  { elem: "GKEF", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outlineAndFill } ] },
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "the complements AG and GE are equal", ref: "I.43" },
                  { claim: "AG is the rectangle AC by CB",        ref: "II.Def.1" } ] },
            { text: "Therefore the sum of {AG} and {GE} equals twice the rectangle {AC} by {CB}.",
              visible: TWICE,
              highlighted: ["ACGH","GKEF"] },
            { text: "But the squares {HF} and {CK} are also the squares on {AC} and {CB}, therefore the sum of the four "
                  + "figures {HF}, {CK}, {AG}, and {GE} equals the sum of the squares on {AC} and {CB} plus twice the "
                  + "rectangle {AC} by {CB}.",
              visible: TWICE,
              highlighted: ["DFGH","CBKG","ACGH","GKEF"] },
            { text: "But {HF}, {CK}, {AG}, and {GE} are the whole {ADEB}, which is the square on {AB}.",
              visible: TWICE,
              // ADEB is this slide's animation target (euclid#171).
              highlighted: ["DFGH","CBKG","ACGH","GKEF"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "ADEB", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outline } ] } },
            { text: "Therefore the square on {AB} equals the sum of the squares on {AC} and {CB} plus twice the rectangle {AC} by {CB}.",
              visible: TWICE,
              highlighted: ["ADEB","DFGH","CBKG","ACGH","GKEF"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "Q.E.D." }] }
        ],
        resolveJustification: function(ref) {
            return (window.eucrefs && window.eucrefs[ref]) || null;
        }
    });

    // Per-page citation lookup for the slideshow's resolveJustification.
    window.eucrefs = (window.eucrefs || {});
    Object.assign(window.eucrefs, {
        "I.5":       "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI5/",
        "I.6":       "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI6/",
        "I.29":      "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI29/",
        "I.31":      "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI31/",
        "I.34":      "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI34/",
        "I.43":      "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI43/",
        "I.46":      "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI46/",
        "I.Def.22":  "/elements/books/bookI/definitions/defI22/",
        "II.Def.1":  "/elements/books/bookII/definitions/defII1/"
    });
</script>
</figure>

Describe the square {ADEB} on {AB}. Join {BD}. Draw {CF} through {C} parallel to either {AD} or {EB}, and draw {HK} through {G} parallel to either {AB} or {DE}. [!just I.46; I.31]

Then, since {CF} is parallel to {AD}, and {BD} falls on them, the exterior angle {CGB} equals the interior and opposite angle {ADB}. [!just I.29]

But the angle {ADB} equals the angle {ABD}, since the side {BA} also equals {AD}. Therefore the angle {CGB} also equals the angle {GBC}, so that the side {BC} also equals the side {CG}. [!just I.5; I.6]

But {CB} equals {GK}, and {CG} to {KB}. Therefore {GK} also equals {KB}. Therefore {CGKB} is equilateral. [!just I.34]

I say next that it is also right-angled.

Since {CG} is parallel to {BK}, the sum of the angles {KBC} and {GCB} equals two right angles. [!just I.29]

But the angle {KBC} is right. Therefore the angle {BCG} is also right, so that the opposite angles {CGK} and {GKB} are also right. [!just I.34]

Therefore {CGKB} is right-angled, and it was also proved equilateral, therefore it is a square, and it is described on {CB}.

For the same reason {HF} is also a square, and it is described on {HG}, that is {AC}.  Therefore the squares {HF} and {KC} are the squares on {AC} and {CB}. [!just I.34]

Now, since {AG} equals {GE}, and {AG} is the rectangle {AC} by {CB}, for {GC} equals {CB}, therefore {GE} also equals the rectangle {AC} by {CB}.  Therefore the sum of {AG} and {GE} equals twice the rectangle {AC} by {CB}. [!just I.43]

But the squares {HF} and {CK} are also the squares on {AC} and {CB}, therefore the sum of the four figures {HF}, {CK}, {AG}, and {GE} equals the sum of the squares on {AC} and {CB} plus twice the rectangle {AC} by {CB}.

But {HF}, {CK}, {AG}, and {GE} are the whole {ADEB}, which is the square on {AB}.

Therefore the square on {AB} equals the the sum of the squares on {AC} and {CB} plus twice the rectangle {AC} by {CB}.

Therefore *if a straight line is cut at random, the square on the whole equals the squares on the segments plus twice the rectangle contained by the segments.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
----
guide:

The statement of the proposition can be interpreted in modern notation as saying that if <span class="math">x = y + z</span>, then <span class="math">x^2 = y^2 + z^2 + 2yz</span>.  More simply, as an identity, it says

<div class="math display">(y + z)^2 = y^2 + z^2 + 2yz</div>

#### Use of this proposition

This is one of the more frequently used propositions of Book II.  It  is used in @II.12 later in this book,  frequently in [Book X](/elements/books/bookX/), and in @XIII.2.  Also, the analogous statement for numbers is used in @IX.15,
